Samba 4.25 (smbclient) for Linux compiled to WebAssembly, the collaboCore guest.

The build (tools/build-samba.sh) cross-compiles with --cross-compile --hostcc=clang-19:
- buildtools/wafsamba/wafsamba.py: --hostcc is stored but nothing used it, so the build's own
  tools (Heimdal's asn1_compile and compile_et, which the build runs) came out as WebAssembly.
  The use_hostcc targets are now compiled and linked with that compiler, without the target's
  CPPFLAGS and LDFLAGS.

No fork(): there is no MMU, so fork() and vfork() do not exist (unistd.h does not declare them).
- lib/replace (and Heimdal's roken.h, which does not include replace.h): rep_fork() fails with
  ENOSYS, so the code that has a fork still compiles and links. None of it is live in smbclient
  (the linker drops rep_fork: background jobs, daemons, smbrun, popen, Heimdal's simple_exec,
  the dns_ex resolver's child), and shell escapes go through system(), musl's posix_spawn.
- tests/fcntl_lock.c, the configure check for fcntl locks, runs its child as the same program
  started again with posix_spawn instead of forking.

No mmap() in the C library:
- lib/util/util.c: anonymous_shared_allocate() is plain zeroed memory. An anonymous shared
  mapping is shared only with children made by fork, which do not exist here.
- tdb is built without robust-mutex locking (it needs the file mapped), so a request for it is
  no longer an error message every time a client opens gencache.tdb (tdb_wrap, dbwrap_open).

Calls through a pointer of another type (WebAssembly checks the type and traps):
- lib/util/stable_sort.c: the two-argument compare function was called as a three-argument
  one; a trampoline takes it as the context.
- libcli/smbreadline/smbreadline.c: the void callback was called as readline's int-returning
  rl_event_hook; a wrapper of the right type. And it is installed for GNU readline only: libedit's
  readline (python/deps', here) calls the hook in a busy loop while it waits for a key and then
  reads the input a byte at a time, which breaks multibyte (UTF-8) input.

